## Solution Analysis

The key challenges here are:

1. **Multiple DML in one transaction** — The safest approach is to always recalculate from the database (within the same transaction), since in an `after` trigger the DML is already applied.
2. **Account trigger recursion** — The existing `AccountTrigger` updates Opportunities when Accounts are updated, which would re-fire our trigger. A `finally`-reset guard handles this cleanly.
3. **Bulk safety** — Collect all affected account IDs, query once, update all accounts in one DML.

File: force-app/main/default/triggers/OpportunityTrigger.trigger
```apex
trigger OpportunityTrigger on Opportunity (after insert, after update, after delete, after undelete) {
    OpportunityPipelineHandler.handle();
}
```

File: force-app/main/default/classes/OpportunityPipelineHandler.cls
```apex
public without sharing class OpportunityPipelineHandler {

    private static Boolean running = false;

    public static void handle() {
        if (running) return;
        running = true;
        try {
            Set<Id> accountIds = collectAffectedAccountIds();
            if (accountIds.isEmpty()) return;
            recalculateAccounts(accountIds);
        } finally {
            running = false;
        }
    }

    private static Set<Id> collectAffectedAccountIds() {
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (Trigger.isInsert) {
            for (Opportunity o : Trigger.new) {
                if (o.IsClosed == false && o.AccountId != null) {
                    accountIds.add(o.AccountId);
                }
            }
        } else if (Trigger.isUpdate) {
            for (Integer i = 0; i < Trigger.new.size(); i++) {
                Opportunity newOpp = Trigger.new[i];
                Opportunity oldOpp = Trigger.old[i];

                if (newOpp.AccountId != oldOpp.AccountId) {
                    // Account moved: both old and new account are affected
                    if (oldOpp.AccountId != null) accountIds.add(oldOpp.AccountId);
                    if (newOpp.AccountId != null) accountIds.add(newOpp.AccountId);
                } else if (newOpp.AccountId != null
                           && (oldOpp.IsClosed != newOpp.IsClosed
                               || oldOpp.Amount != newOpp.Amount)) {
                    // Same account, but open/closed status or amount changed
                    accountIds.add(newOpp.AccountId);
                }
            }
        } else if (Trigger.isDelete) {
            for (Opportunity o : Trigger.old) {
                if (o.IsClosed == false && o.AccountId != null) {
                    accountIds.add(o.AccountId);
                }
            }
        } else if (Trigger.isUndelete) {
            for (Opportunity o : Trigger.new) {
                if (o.IsClosed == false && o.AccountId != null) {
                    accountIds.add(o.AccountId);
                }
            }
        }

        return accountIds;
    }

    private static void recalculateAccounts(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        // Query the current transactional state so that prior DML in the
        // same transaction is already reflected.
        Map<Id, Decimal> pipelineMap = new Map<Id, Decimal>();
        Map<Id, Integer> countMap = new Map<Id, Integer>();

        for (Opportunity o : [
            SELECT AccountId, Amount
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds AND IsClosed = false
        ]) {
            Decimal amt = o.Amount != null ? o.Amount : 0;
            pipelineMap.put(o.AccountId, (pipelineMap.get(o.AccountId) ?? 0) + amt);
            countMap.put(o.AccountId, countMap.get(o.AccountId) + 1);
        }

        List<Account> accountsToUpdate = new List<Account>();

        for (Id accountId : accountIds) {
            Account acc = new Account(Id = accountId);
            acc.FB_Open_Pipeline__c = pipelineMap.get(accountId) ?? 0;
            acc.FB_Open_Opportunities__c = countMap.get(accountId) ?? 0;
            accountsToUpdate.add(acc);
        }

        if (!accountsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update accountsToUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

### Why this works

| Concern | How it's addressed |
|---|---|
| **Multiple DML in one tx** | We always query the DB for the current state of the affected accounts' open opps. Since Apex triggers run *after* DML is applied within the transaction, the query sees all prior changes. |
| **Account trigger recursion** | The `running` flag (reset in `finally`) prevents re-entry. When the `AccountTrigger` updates opps (rating), our trigger fires but immediately returns. Once the whole call stack unwinds, the flag is `false` again for any subsequent DML. |
| **Account move** | Both the old and new `AccountId` are added to the set, so both accounts get recalculated. |
| **Last open opp removed** | The query returns no rows for that account; `?? 0` ensures the fields are set to `0` (not blank). |
| **Bulk (200 records)** | One SOQL query + one `update` DML regardless of batch size. The `Set<Id>` deduplicates accounts naturally. |
| **Null Amount** | Treated as `0` in the sum. |

